select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-b08-021","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"List the six steps in CPM project planning.","back":"1) Specify individual activities, 2) determine their sequence, 3) draw the network diagram, 4) estimate each activity's completion time, 5) identify the critical path (longest path), 6) update the diagram as the project progresses.","explain":"The activity list comes from the WBS, and the project should have a single start and a single finish activity. Updating can reveal a new critical path.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p229","order":1400},
{"id":"p1-b08-022","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"True or false: CPM is a probabilistic model that uses three time estimates for each activity.","back":"False. CPM is deterministic and uses one fixed time per activity; PERT is the probabilistic model with three estimates.","explain":"CPM time estimates come from past experience or knowledgeable people and ignore variation. PERT uses optimistic, most likely and pessimistic times.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p229","order":1401},
{"id":"p1-b08-023","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"Define ES, EF, LS and LF, and state the formulas linking them.","back":"ES: earliest start once predecessors finish. EF = ES + t. LF: latest finish without delaying the project. LS = LF − t.","explain":"ES and EF come from a forward pass from the start; LS and LF from a backward pass from the finish. Slack = LS − ES = LF − EF; on the critical path ES = LS and EF = LF.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p230","order":1402},
{"id":"p1-b08-024","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"State the earliest-start rule (forward pass) and the latest-finish rule (backward pass) at a node.","back":"Earliest start of an activity leaving a node = the LARGEST EF of all activities entering it. Latest finish of an activity entering a node = the SMALLEST LS of all activities leaving it.","explain":"Mixing up largest and smallest is a classic error. The forward pass waits for the slowest predecessor; the backward pass must not delay the earliest-needed successor.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p232","order":1403},
{"id":"p1-b08-025","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"What is project crashing, and where should resources be added or saved?","back":"Crashing is shortening the project by adding resources to critical-path activities. To speed up, add resources only to critical activities; to save resources, lengthen only non-critical activities, within their float.","explain":"Shortening a non-critical activity does not shorten the project, because the critical path sets the minimum completion time.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p233","order":1404},
{"id":"p1-b08-026","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"When and for what project was PERT developed, and what kind of model is it?","back":"In the late 1950s for the US Navy's Polaris project, which had thousands of contractors. It is a probabilistic network model that allows for randomness in activity times.","explain":"PERT steps are like CPM except for time estimation: it uses optimistic, most likely and pessimistic times and assumes a beta distribution for them.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p233","order":1405},
{"id":"p1-b08-027","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"Give the PERT formulas for expected time, standard deviation and variance of an activity. How is the project's variance found?","back":"Tₑ = (Tₒ + 4Tₘ + Tₚ)/6; σ = (Tₚ − Tₒ)/6; V = σ². Project variance = sum of the variances of the activities on the critical path.","explain":"Assuming the critical-path duration is normally distributed (reasonable when it has enough activities), this variance gives the probability of finishing by a given date.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p234","order":1406},
{"id":"p1-b08-028","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"What information does PERT provide?","back":"Expected project completion time; probability of completion before a specified date; the critical activities; activities with slack that can lend resources to critical ones; and activity start and end dates.","explain":"The PERT chart is not final; estimated times are replaced by actual times as the project unfolds, and the chart is revised if delays need extra resources.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p236","order":1407},
{"id":"p1-b08-029","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"What are the limitations of PERT, and why does it tend to underestimate project duration?","back":"Time estimates are subjective; the real distribution may not be beta; and PERT assumes the project completion time distribution equals that of the critical path. Since other paths can become critical if delayed, PERT consistently underestimates the expected completion time.","explain":"Near-critical paths with high variance can end up longer than the expected critical path, which a single-path calculation ignores.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p236","order":1408},
{"id":"p1-b08-030","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"Before implementing a project, what should top management identify, and how should each be handled?","back":"Strengths and weaknesses (internal forces) and opportunities and threats (external forces). Strengths and opportunities should be exploited; weaknesses and threats must be overcome.","explain":"Monitoring should be in place before implementation starts, appear on the work plan and involve all stakeholders. The implementation plan should include a schedule or timeline, such as the book's boiler milestones from component delivery to testing and commissioning.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p237","order":1409},
{"id":"p1-b08-031","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"What are the three categories of project costs? Give an example of each.","back":"Human resource costs (salaries and benefits of project staff); administrative costs (everyday support costs such as local telephone, copier paper, heating, support staff); resource costs (materials, equipment leases, long-distance calls, travel – specific to the project).","explain":"The budget is used throughout the project to compare money actually spent with estimates for each activity.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p237","order":1410},
{"id":"p1-b08-032","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish direct and indirect project costs, with examples.","back":"Direct costs can be attributed directly to project work, e.g. team salaries, equipment rentals, team training. Indirect costs are not specific to the project, e.g. lease of a building shared with other staff, or administrative/procurement staff who help but have no assigned tasks.","explain":"Each organisation has its own way of accounting for indirect costs; the finance or accounts department advises how to include them in the budget.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p238","order":1411},
{"id":"p1-b08-033","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"What is the difference between analogous estimating and bottom-up estimating?","back":"Analogous: estimate based on the actual costs of previous similar projects of comparable size and scope. Bottom-up: estimate each task individually and add them up.","explain":"Bottom-up estimating uses the WBS work packages. Inadequate budget is one reason projects fail; the finance manager usually sets the cost and the project manager negotiates within it.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p238","order":1412},
{"id":"p1-b08-034","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"What is project construction (commissioning), and to which projects does it apply?","back":"The process of assuring that all systems and components are designed, installed, tested, operated and maintained to the user's operational requirements. It applies to new projects and to existing units being expanded, renovated or revamped.","explain":"Commissioning checks, inspects and tests every component from individual instruments up to subsystems and systems, and spans design, procurement, construction, assembly and handover, sometimes including assisted operation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p239","order":1413},
{"id":"p1-b08-035","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"fact","front":"Give the general M&V equation for energy savings. What does the adjustments term do, and what conditions does it usually cover?","back":"Energy savings = Base-year energy use − Post-retrofit energy use ± Adjustments. Adjustments bring both periods to the same conditions, commonly weather, occupancy, plant throughput and related equipment operation.","explain":"Adjustments come from identifiable physical facts and can be routine (weather) or as needed (e.g. a second shift added). M&V is central when contract payments or guarantees depend on the size of savings.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p239","order":1414},
{"id":"p1-b08-036","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"problem","front":"An activity has an optimistic time of 15 days, most likely 18 days and pessimistic 27 days. Find its PERT expected time, standard deviation and variance.","back":"Tₑ = 19 days; σ = 2 days; V = 4 days².","explain":"The expected time is pulled above the most likely value because the pessimistic estimate is far from it. (A simpler book question with 10, 15, 20 days gives exactly 15 days, because the estimates are symmetric.)","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p243","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the expected-time formula.","answer":"Tₑ = (Tₒ + 4Tₘ + Tₚ)/6","explain":"Weighted average for a beta distribution."},{"prompt":"Calculate Tₑ.","answer":"(15 + 4 × 18 + 27)/6 = 114/6 = 19 days","explain":"Not the simple average (20) or the most likely (18)."},{"prompt":"Standard deviation.","answer":"σ = (27 − 15)/6 = 2 days","explain":"One-sixth of the range."},{"prompt":"Variance.","answer":"V = 2² = 4","explain":"Variances add along a path; standard deviations do not."}],"order":1415},
{"id":"p1-b08-037","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"problem","front":"CPM, weeks (duration; predecessors): A 5 (–), B 6 (–), C 4 (A), D 3 (A), E 1 (A), F 4 (E), G 14 (D, F), H 12 (B, C), I 2 (G, H). Find ES/EF, LS/LF, slack, the critical path and the project duration.","back":"Critical path A–E–F–G–I, 26 weeks. Slack: B 6, C 3, D 2, H 3; A, E, F, G, I have zero slack.","explain":"The activity durations add up to 51 weeks, but parallel work (e.g. A and B together) cuts the project to 26 weeks. Non-critical activities can be delayed up to their slack without delaying completion.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p230","steps":[{"prompt":"Forward pass (EF = ES + t; ES = largest EF of predecessors).","answer":"A 0–5, B 0–6, C 5–9, D 5–8, E 5–6, F 6–10, G 10–24 (max of D 8, F 10), H 9–21 (max of B 6, C 9), I 24–26","explain":"The project finishes at EF of I = 26 weeks."},{"prompt":"Backward pass (LS = LF − t; LF = smallest LS of successors).","answer":"I 24–26, G 10–24, H 12–24, F 6–10, D 7–10, E 5–6, C 8–12, B 6–12, A 0–5 (LF = min of C 8, D 7, E 5)","explain":"Start from LF = 26 for the last activity."},{"prompt":"Slack = LS − ES.","answer":"A 0, B 6, C 3, D 2, E 0, F 0, G 0, H 3, I 0","explain":"E.g. C: 8 − 5 = 3 weeks."},{"prompt":"Critical path and duration.","answer":"A–E–F–G–I: 5 + 1 + 4 + 14 + 2 = 26 weeks","explain":"The zero-slack activities form the longest path."}],"order":1416},
{"id":"p1-b08-038","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"problem","front":"PERT, expected times in days (predecessors): a 20 (–), b 20 (–), c 10 (–), d 15 (a), e 10 (b, c), f 14 (b, c), g 4 (b, c), h 11 (c), i 18 (g, h), j 8 (d, e). Find ES/EF, LS/LF, slack and the critical path.","back":"Critical path a–d–j, 43 days. Slack: b 1, c 4, e 5, f 9, g 1, h 4, i 1; a, d, j zero.","explain":"Once expected times are found from the three estimates, the PERT network is worked exactly like CPM. Path b–g–i (42 days) has only 1 day of slack, so it could easily become critical if b, g or i slips.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p234","steps":[{"prompt":"Forward pass.","answer":"a 0–20, b 0–20, c 0–10, d 20–35, e 20–30, f 20–34, g 20–24, h 10–21, i 24–42 (max of g 24, h 21), j 35–43 (max of d 35, e 30)","explain":"Project end = latest EF = 43 (j); f and i also end the project."},{"prompt":"Backward pass from 43.","answer":"j 35–43, i 25–43, f 29–43, d 20–35, e 25–35, g 21–25, h 14–25, a 0–20, b 1–21 (min LS of e 25, f 29, g 21), c 4–14 (min LS of e, f, g, h 14)","explain":"LF = smallest LS of successors."},{"prompt":"Slack = LS − ES.","answer":"a 0, b 1, c 4, d 0, e 5, f 9, g 1, h 4, i 1, j 0","explain":"Zero slack marks critical activities."},{"prompt":"Critical path.","answer":"a–d–j: 20 + 15 + 8 = 43 days","explain":"The longest path through the network."}],"order":1417},
{"id":"p1-b08-039","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"problem","front":"Expected times in weeks (predecessors): A 7 (–), B 9 (A), C 12 (A), D 2 (B), E 10 (D, C), F 3 (C), G 2 (F), H 8 (E, G), I 2 (H). List all paths with their durations and identify the critical path.","back":"A–B–D–E–H–I = 38; A–C–E–H–I = 39; A–C–F–G–H–I = 34. Critical path A–C–E–H–I, 39 weeks.","explain":"Listing every start-to-finish path and adding durations is a quick way to find the critical path in small networks. The longest path sets the minimum project time.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 08 p240","steps":[{"prompt":"Trace the paths from A to I.","answer":"A–B–D–E–H–I, A–C–E–H–I, A–C–F–G–H–I","explain":"E needs both D and C; H needs both E and G, so three routes reach I."},{"prompt":"Path 1 duration.","answer":"7 + 9 + 2 + 10 + 8 + 2 = 38 weeks","explain":"Sum of expected times."},{"prompt":"Path 2 duration.","answer":"7 + 12 + 10 + 8 + 2 = 39 weeks","explain":"Sum of expected times."},{"prompt":"Path 3 duration.","answer":"7 + 12 + 3 + 2 + 8 + 2 = 34 weeks","explain":"Sum of expected times."},{"prompt":"Critical path.","answer":"A–C–E–H–I, 39 weeks","explain":"Longest path = expected project duration."}],"order":1418},
{"id":"p1-083","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What principle is energy Monitoring & Targeting (M&T) based on?","back":"'You can't manage what you don't measure.' Energy use is related to its driver (production, degree-days), and actual consumption is compared with expected consumption so that deviations can be found and acted on.","explain":"The M&T elements are recording (measuring), analysing (correlating with production), comparing (against a baseline or target), setting targets, monitoring, reporting and controlling. M&T turns energy from an overhead into a managed variable cost.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q50","order":1419},
{"id":"p1-084","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"How is fixed (non-production) energy consumption found from data?","back":"Plot energy (y) against production (x) on an XY scatter chart and fit E = m·P + c. The intercept c is the fixed energy consumption, and the slope m is the variable energy per unit of production.","explain":"Fixed energy (lighting, HVAC, idle running, losses) is used regardless of output. That is why SEC rises at low capacity utilisation. Reducing c is often the cheapest saving.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q17","order":1420},
{"id":"p1-085","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"Why is an energy baseline established in M&T?","back":"To fix a reference point (expected energy for a given production) against which later energy performance improvements are measured.","explain":"The baseline is usually a regression E = mP + c from a representative period before the energy-saving measures. The baseline and energy performance indicators are core requirements of ISO 50001 and of M&V for ESCO contracts.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q19","order":1421},
{"id":"p1-086","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What is CUSUM, and how do you interpret its graph?","back":"CUSUM = cumulative sum of (actual − predicted) energy, period by period. A falling line means savings (actual below predicted). A rising line means excess consumption. A horizontal line means actual = predicted (no change in performance).","explain":"Predicted energy comes from the baseline equation, e.g. E = 2.2·P + 10,000. The final CUSUM value gives the total savings (if negative) over the period. Changes in slope mark when performance changed, for example when a measure was implemented or a fault appeared.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q43, L4; BEE 2024 P1 L1","order":1422},
{"id":"p1-087","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"problem","front":"The baseline relationship for a plant is E = 120·P + 20,000 (E in kWh, P in tonnes). After an energy-saving programme, the monthly data (production in t / actual energy in kWh) are: Apr 400/66,000; May 420/70,000; Jun 380/63,000; Jul 450/73,500; Aug 410/67,000; Sep 390/64,500. Build the CUSUM table and find the total savings.","back":"CUSUM after six months = −10,000 kWh, i.e. 10,000 kWh saved.","explain":"For each month, predicted energy comes from the baseline equation. The monthly difference (actual − predicted) is added up cumulatively. A steadily falling CUSUM line confirms sustained savings; a flat stretch would mean performance had returned to baseline.","steps":[{"prompt":"Predicted energy each month (120P + 20,000).","answer":"Apr 68,000; May 70,400; Jun 65,600; Jul 74,000; Aug 69,200; Sep 66,800 kWh","explain":"Use the baseline regression."},{"prompt":"Difference (actual − predicted).","answer":"Apr −2,000; May −400; Jun −2,600; Jul −500; Aug −2,200; Sep −2,300","explain":"Negative = consumed less than expected."},{"prompt":"Cumulative sum.","answer":"−2,000; −2,400; −5,000; −5,500; −7,700; −10,000","explain":"CUSUMₙ = CUSUMₙ₋₁ + (Eₐ − Eₚ)."},{"prompt":"Interpret.","answer":"Total savings over 6 months = 10,000 kWh; the CUSUM line slopes down throughout.","explain":"A downward slope means savings; a horizontal line means no change."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 L4; BEE 2024 P1 L1 (modified)","order":1423},
{"id":"p1-b09-001","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"Why is an energy audit alone not enough to keep control of a plant's energy consumption?","back":"An audit gives only a 'picture' of past consumption. A monitoring programme is needed to keep control, and it must be paired with targeting (setting targets and making planned improvements) to actually improve performance.","explain":"The book stresses that monitoring by itself only records consumption and so has limited value. Improvement comes when targets are set and actual use is compared against them, which is why the technique is always 'Monitoring and Targeting' together.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p247","order":1424},
{"id":"p1-b09-002","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"By what typical percentage have M&T programmes reduced annual energy costs in industry?","back":"Typically 5–15% of annual energy costs.","explain":"BEE Book 1 cites typical reductions in annual energy costs of 5 to 15% across various industrial sectors from M&T programmes. The savings come mainly from better management control rather than capital investment.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p247","order":1425},
{"id":"p1-b09-003","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"Under M&T, how are plant utilities such as fuel, steam, compressed air, water and electricity treated?","back":"As controllable resources, managed the same way as raw materials, finished-product inventory, personnel and capital.","explain":"M&T is primarily a management technique that uses energy information to eliminate waste, reduce and control current energy use and improve operating procedures. It combines the principles of energy use and statistics, so energy stops being treated as a fixed overhead.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p247","order":1426},
{"id":"p1-b09-004","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish 'monitoring' from 'targeting' as defined in the BEE guide book.","back":"Monitoring establishes the existing pattern of energy consumption and explains deviations from it. Targeting identifies a desirable consumption level and works towards achieving it.","explain":"Targets are based on historical data (average or best) collected during monitoring, and on benchmarking against similar organisations. Monitoring keeps the existing pattern under control; targeting moves it to a better level.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p247","order":1427},
{"id":"p1-b09-005","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What are Energy Account Centres (EACs), and who should be accountable for them?","back":"EACs are departments, processes or cost centres set up for energy accounting before starting M&T. The operational manager responsible for each EAC is accountable for its energy consumption.","explain":"Setting up EACs is the first organisational step in M&T. It assigns energy use to the people who control it, so variances can be traced to an area and to a manager.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p247","order":1428},
{"id":"p1-b09-006","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"M&T schemes are classified by number of sites and level of metering. Name the four types and how EACs are formed in each.","back":"1) Single site, central utility metering: treat as one EAC. 2) Single site with sub-metering: split into several EACs. 3) Multi-site, central metering at each site: each site is a separate EAC. 4) Multi-site with sub-metering: divide each site into several EACs.","explain":"The level of metering decides how finely energy can be allocated. Sub-metering is what allows a site to be broken into several accountable centres.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p248","order":1429},
{"id":"p1-b09-007","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"List the six key elements of a Monitoring & Targeting system.","back":"Recording, Analysing & comparing, Setting targets, Monitoring, Reporting, and Controlling.","explain":"Recording collects reliable energy data for each EAC. Analysing relates it to output to get standard performance. Targets improve on that standard. Monitoring compares actual use with target. Reporting tells management about variances, and Controlling corrects them.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p248","order":1430},
{"id":"p1-b09-008","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"How is 'standard energy performance' of an EAC established, and what if historical data does not exist?","back":"By regression analysis relating energy use to a measured output (e.g. production) over 12–24 months of historical data. If no data exists, an energy audit is done to establish it.","explain":"Standard energy performance is the baseline for judging future performance and can serve as the initial target. Achievable targets are then set to improve on it, based on external benchmarking or the organisation's own best (least) historical consumption.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p248","order":1431},
{"id":"p1-b09-009","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"Name five things an M&T system particularly involves.","back":"Checking the accuracy of energy invoices; allocating energy costs to departments (EACs); determining energy performance/efficiency; recording energy use so efficiency projects can be checked for results; highlighting performance problems in equipment or systems.","explain":"These are the routine functions that make M&T a management tool. Invoice checking and cost allocation are accounting tasks; performance tracking and fault highlighting drive technical action.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p249","order":1432},
{"id":"p1-b09-010","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"Besides lower energy cost, name five benefits of M&T.","back":"Any five: explain increases or decreases in energy use; show trends (weekly, seasonal, operational); improve energy budgeting against production plans; see how the organisation reacted to past changes; predict future use when planning changes; diagnose specific areas of waste; develop performance targets for energy action plans; manage energy instead of accepting it as a fixed cost.","explain":"The ultimate goal of M&T is reducing energy cost through better efficiency and management control. The other benefits mostly come from having a reliable energy–production relationship that can be used to explain, predict and plan.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p249","order":1433},
{"id":"p1-b09-011","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"At what four levels can energy information be obtained, and from what sources?","back":"Plant level: financial accounting systems (utilities cost centre). Plant department level: comparative data for similar facilities, service-entrance meter readings. System level (e.g. compressor house): sub-metering. Equipment level: nameplate data, run-time and schedules, sub-meters on specific equipment.","explain":"All these data can be processed to yield information about facility performance. The finer the level, the more useful the data is for finding where energy is wasted.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p249","order":1434},
{"id":"p1-b09-012","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What standard conversion factors to kcal does Table 9.1 use for electricity, HSD, furnace oil and LPG?","back":"Electricity 860 kcal/kWh; HSD 10,500 kcal/kg; furnace oil 10,200 kcal/kg; LPG 12,000 kcal/kg.","explain":"For the annual-consumption analysis, all energy is first converted to a common unit (usually kcal). The data is then tabulated and shown as percentage breakdowns and pie charts of energy share and cost share.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p250","order":1435},
{"id":"p1-b09-013","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What is the main limitation of the annual energy consumption percentage-breakdown (pie chart) analysis?","back":"It makes no allowance for variable factors that influence energy use (e.g. production, climatic zone or occupancy of buildings), so it cannot be used to compare different organisations.","explain":"The analysis is quick and shows energy and cost breakdown and trends. In the book's example, furnace oil was 62% of energy but 66% of cost, while electricity was 18% of energy but 20% of cost. It still says nothing about why consumption changed.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p251","order":1436},
{"id":"p1-b09-014","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What does a time-dependent energy analysis (energy plotted against month) show, and what are its limitations?","back":"It shows general trends, seasonal patterns and base load, and lets exceptions be spotted immediately. But it cannot explain why trends occur, and it is only a comparative tool, not an absolute one.","explain":"In the book's example, the base load was about 10,500 kWh/month, and a rise in Nov–Jan pointed to loss of control over electricity use. Finding the reason needs further analysis, such as relating energy to production. A bar chart of current year vs previous year has the same weakness: it does not clearly show trends.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p252","order":1437},
{"id":"p1-b09-015","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What is a norm chart, and what is it good for?","back":"A sequential plot of actual energy consumption overlaid on target consumption. It has little analytical value but is useful for highlighting exceptions and communicating them to managers.","explain":"Because a norm chart is a simple historical record, senior and operational managers find it easy to understand. It is a communication tool rather than a diagnostic one.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p253","order":1438},
{"id":"p1-b09-016","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"In a deviance chart, how is a month plotted when actual consumption exceeds target, and why are limit lines added?","back":"Deviance = actual − target, so higher-than-target use plots as a positive value and lower use as negative. Upper and lower limit lines separate normal variation from serious deviations.","explain":"Deviance charts are particularly good at highlighting problems so remedial action can be taken, and they can trigger detailed exception reports.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p254","order":1439}
]$cards$::jsonb);
